{id:"BIO-002",exam:["UGC NET"],subject:"Biomechanics",topic:"Levers",difficulty:"Hard",type:"MCQ",year:2024,
question:"Most lever systems in the human body (e.g., elbow flexion by the biceps) are classified as which class of lever?",
options:["First Class","Second Class","Third Class","Fourth Class"],correctIndex:2,
explanation:"Most human body levers are Third Class levers, where the effort (muscle force) is between the fulcrum (joint) and the resistance (load) — sacrificing mechanical advantage for greater speed/range of motion.",
concept:{title:"Classes of Levers in the Body",definition:"Levers are classified by the relative position of fulcrum, effort, and resistance.",points:["1st Class: fulcrum between effort & resistance (e.g., head nodding at atlanto-occipital joint)","2nd Class: resistance between fulcrum & effort (e.g., calf raise — rare in the body)","3rd Class: effort between fulcrum & resistance (most common — e.g., elbow flexion)"],memoryTrick:"'3rd Class = most common, built for speed not force'.",sportsRelevance:"Explains why the human arm is fast but not maximally strong — a biomechanical trade-off."},
relatedIds:["BIO-001","ANA-003"],source:"Biomechanics of Sport - Hall",tags:["biomechanics","levers"]}
